```java
public class OrderService {
    // This counter lives in Metaspace, not heap
    private static int totalOrders = 0;

    private String orderId;  // Instance field — on heap

    public OrderService(String orderId) {
        this.orderId = orderId;
        totalOrders++;  // Shared across all instances
    }

    public static int getTotalOrders() {
        return totalOrders;  // Static method accesses Metaspace data
    }
}

// Usage
var service1 = new OrderService("ORD-001");  // service1 on heap
var service2 = new OrderService("ORD-002");  // service2 on heap
System.out.println(OrderService.getTotalOrders());  // Prints 2
// totalOrders itself is in Metaspace, shared by both instances
```

```java
// Compile this, then inspect the bytecode with:  javap -c Greeter
public class Greeter {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String msg = "Hi from bytecode";  // javac produces .class bytecode
        System.out.println(msg);           // same .class runs on ANY JVM
    }
}

// javap -c Greeter shows JVM bytecode (not CPU machine code), e.g.:
//   0: ldc           #7   // String Hi from bytecode
//   2: astore_1
//   3: getstatic     #9   // Field java/lang/System.out
//   6: aload_1
//   7: invokevirtual #15  // Method println
// The JIT later turns hot bytecode into native x86/ARM machine code.
```
Bytecode is portable and JVM-targeted; machine code is CPU-specific and produced only at runtime by the JIT.

```java
public class HelloWorld {
    // Step 3 (prepare): JVM allocates memory for counter, sets to 0
    private static int counter = 42;

    // Step 4 (initialization): This runs, setting counter to 42
    static {
        System.out.println("Static block executed");
        counter = 100;  // Now counter becomes 100
    }

    // Step 5: main method frame pushed to thread stack
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Step 6: Execution Engine interprets or JIT-compiles
        System.out.println("Counter: " + counter);
        var message = "Hello, World!";
        System.out.println(message);
    }
}

// Output:
// Static block executed
// Counter: 100
// Hello, World!
